\lispPackageChapter{livermore/multiplexer}

The \texttt{livermore/multiplexer} package is the Boolean multiplexer used as
Wilson's classic single-step XCS problem~\cite{wilson-1995}.  An
\texttt{address-width} of $k$ takes a bit vector of length
$k + 2^k$: $k$ address bits and $2^k$ data bits.  The output is the data bit
named by the address.

\section{Functions}

\lispFunctionSubsection{random-bit}

Returns $0$ or $1$.

\lispFunctionSubsection{random-bit-vector}

A random bit vector of the given positive length.

\lispFunctionSubsection{binary-decoder}

Decodes a sequence of bits as an unsigned integer.  \texttt{nil} or $0$ is a
zero bit; anything else is a one bit.

\lispFunctionSubsection{multiplexer-length}

The bit-vector length for a given address width: $k + 2^k$.

\lispFunctionSubsection{multiplexer}

The multiplexer output.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(multiplexer \lispVar{address-width} \lispVar{bits})}

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(multiplexer 2 #*110001) ; address 3 selects the last data bit => 1
(multiplexer 1 #*010)    ; address 0 selects the first data bit => 1
\end{lstlisting}

\lispFunctionSubsection{truth-vector}

A general vector of \texttt{t} and \texttt{nil} corresponding to the $1$ and
$0$ bits of a bit vector.
